      "message": "Here\u0027s an analysis by Claude Opus 4.7 via Copilot\n\nLooks like there\u0027s a few critical issues that are definitely things that should be fixed before we can move forward with something like this. I\u0027m going to leave this comment as unresolved so it can be worked through easier for now.\n\n--ajg\n\n---\n\n# Security Evaluation: `feat/parrotbot-dependabot-relay`\n\nRead-only review of the diff against `main`. The change adds\n`parrotbot.py` (relays `@parrot @\u003cbot\u003e \u003ccmd\u003e` from a Gerrit comment to\na linked GitHub PR), wires it into `comment_added.py`, and adds\n`pygerrit2` as a dependency.\n\nFiles changed:\n\n- `README.rst` (+69)\n- `pyproject.toml` (+1) — adds `pygerrit2~\u003d2.0`\n- `src/gerrit_to_platform/comment_added.py` (+12) — dispatches parrot\n  commands before the normal workflow flow\n- `src/gerrit_to_platform/parrotbot.py` (+416, new)\n- `tests/test_parrotbot.py` (+539, new)\n\n## 🔴 Critical / High\n\n### 1. Cross-repository command relay via attacker-controlled trailer\n\nIn `extract_github_pr_from_commit`, the linked PR is taken from the\n`GitHub-PR:` trailer in the change\u0027s commit message. The commit\nmessage is fully controlled by the change author. A committer of\nproject **X** can craft (or amend) a change with:\n\n```\nGitHub-PR: https://github.com/\u003cany-org\u003e/\u003cany-repo\u003e/pull/\u003cN\u003e\n```\n\nand then trigger `@parrot @dependabot merge` — the bot will dispatch\nthe merge command to an unrelated repository where the bot token has\npush access.\n\n**Fix:** bind the trailer to an allow-list mapping\n`gerrit-project → github org/repo` (config-driven) and reject if the\nPR\u0027s `owner/repo` is not in that mapping. Bonus: verify the PR\u0027s head\nSHA matches the Gerrit change\u0027s commit (the GitHub2Gerrit pipeline\nalready records this linkage).\n\n### 2. `target_bot` is unvalidated — arbitrary `@mention` spam / social engineering\n\nThe regex `@(\\w[\\w\\-\\[\\]]*)` accepts any GitHub handle, and\n`relay_comment \u003d f\"@{target_bot} {command}\"` posts it on the GitHub\nPR. A committer could use the bot to fire `@\u003cany-user\u003e close`,\n`@\u003csecurity-team\u003e merge`, etc., from the bot account — useful for\nimpersonation/spam — and the action is logged on GitHub as the bot,\nnot the human.\n\n**Fix:** whitelist `target_bot` (e.g.,\n`{\"dependabot\", \"renovate\", \"github-actions\"}`).\n\n### 3. State-changing commands under bot identity (attribution loss)\n\nCombined with #1/#2, relayed actions like `merge`, `squash`, `close`,\n`reopen` are attributed to the bot\u0027s GitHub account, not the\noriginating Gerrit committer. The audit trail on the GitHub side is\nlost; only the optional Gerrit confirmation comment ties it back.\n\n**Fix:** at minimum include the originating Gerrit username and\nchange URL in the relayed GitHub comment body, e.g.\n\n```\nRelay requested by `\u003cgerrit-user\u003e` from \u003cchange_url\u003e:\n@dependabot recreate\n```\n\nConsider splitting the whitelist into \"safe\" (`recreate`, `rebase`)\nvs \"privileged\" (`merge`, `squash`, `close`) and gating the latter\nbehind tighter authorization / config.\n\n## 🟠 Medium\n\n### 4. Unauthenticated `GerritRestAPI(url\u003d...)`\n\n`check_user_authorized`, `extract_github_pr_from_commit`, and\n`post_gerrit_comment` all instantiate `GerritRestAPI` with no\nauthentication. `GET /groups/{uuid}/members/` and\n`POST /changes/.../review` typically require an authenticated\naccount. As written the authorization check will fail for every\ngroup (caught in the broad `except`, falls through, returns\n`False`). That fails closed — so security isn\u0027t immediately violated\n— but the bot is functionally a no-op for any non-public path. The\nbigger risk is a future operator \"fixing\" this by loosening Gerrit\nACLs rather than authenticating. The Gerrit confirmation post will\nalso silently fail.\n\n**Fix:** load Gerrit credentials from config (mirroring the\n`github.com` token pattern) and pass an authenticated\n`HTTPBasicAuthFromNetrc` / `HTTPBasicAuth` to `GerritRestAPI`.\nValidate at startup.\n\n### 5. Regex matches anywhere in the comment, including quoted replies\n\n`PARROT_RE.search(comment)` fires on `\u003e @parrot @dependabot recreate`\nquoted from a prior reply. A committer who simply quotes an earlier\ncomment unintentionally re-triggers the bot.\n\n**Fix:** iterate line-by-line and skip lines starting with `\u003e` (or\nanchor with `(?m)^\\s*@parrot...`).\n\n### 6. Project name not URL-encoded\n\n`encoded_project \u003d project.replace(\"/\", \"%2F\")` only encodes `/`.\nCharacters like `%`, `?`, `#`, `..`, or stray whitespace are not.\nGerrit project names are usually constrained, but path traversal /\ninjection into the `/projects/.../access` endpoint is theoretically\npossible if a hostile project name ever exists.\n\n**Fix:** `urllib.parse.quote(project, safe\u003d\"\")`.\n\n### 7. `change_number` flows unsanitised into URL paths\n\n`/changes/{change_number}/detail` and\n`/changes/{change_number}/revisions/current/review` interpolate\n`change_number` directly. It is sourced from\n`get_change_number(change_url)`; if that parser doesn\u0027t enforce\ndigits-only, path injection is theoretically possible.\n\n**Fix:** assert `change_number.isdigit()` (or\n`re.fullmatch(r\"\\d+\", change_number)`) before using it.\n\n### 8. No rate limiting / idempotency\n\nRe-posting the same Gerrit comment retriggers the relay. Useful as\nDoS against the GitHub API or as noise on a PR. Adversarially-quoted\ncomments compound this.\n\n**Fix:** dedupe per `(change, revision, command)` or rate-limit per\nuser.\n\n## 🟡 Low / Informational\n\n### 9. Single broad GitHub token\n\n`get_setting(\"github.com\", \"token\")` returns a single token whose\nwrite power on behalf of any project committer extends across every\nrepository it can reach. Document this and prefer a dedicated,\nscoped token (or a GitHub App installation per-org).\n\n### 10. Enabled check exception class\n\nThe \"enabled\" check uses `except (KeyError, TypeError)`. Depending\non `get_setting`\u0027s implementation (configparser-based elsewhere in\nthe repo), `NoSectionError` / `NoOptionError` may not be subclasses\nof `KeyError`. Worth confirming so a missing `[parrotbot]` section\ndisables the feature cleanly instead of raising and aborting the\nhook.\n\n### 11. Broad `except Exception` blocks\n\nEvery helper swallows errors with only a log line — generally\nfail-closed here (returns `False` / `None`), which is acceptable,\nbut makes operational debugging harder. Consider narrowing to\n`RequestException` / `HTTPError`.\n\n### 12. Logging\n\nRecords the first 80 chars of the relay comment plus usernames and\nchange numbers — no secrets logged. Looks fine.\n\n### 13. Trailer regex strictness\n\nMatches only `https://github.com/...` — won\u0027t accept `www.` or\n`http://`. That\u0027s actually a small *plus* (no scheme/host\nsmuggling).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe biggest concerns are:\n\n1. **Trailer-driven PR target with no project↔repo binding** (#1)\n2. **Open-ended `target_bot` capture** (#2)\n3. **Loss of attribution on state-changing GitHub commands** (#3)\n\nAddressing those — an allow-list mapping, a bot whitelist, and\noriginator metadata in the relayed comment — makes the design\ndefensible. The unauthenticated Gerrit REST calls (#4) are a\nfunctional / operational gap that risks an unsafe \"loosen ACLs\" fix\nlater. Items 5–8 are tractable hardening. No secrets are leaked and\nthe authorization check fails closed in its current broken state.",
